<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="steviol biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>The diterpenoid |FRAME:CPD-14501| is the aglycone precursor of the sweet compounds produced by the plant |FRAME:TAX-55670|, and is thus the entry compound in the steviol glucoside biosynthesis pathway. Steviol has also been reported to show promise as cancer preventive agent in various &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; anticancer tests |CITS:[19131254]|, and to offer therapeuthic benefits through anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory actions |CITS:[19000919]|. In the plants, steviol is involved in the regulation of growth processes |CITS:[18329674]|, but is generally considered less efficient than the corresponding glucosides (see |FRAME:PWY-7071|). Up to the formation of |FRAME: CPD1F-132|, the |FRAME:PWY-7070| pathway shares its reactions with the biosynthesis of the diterpenoid plant hormone |FRAME:CPD1F-95| (see |FRAME:PWY-5034|). Therefore, the C-13 hydroxylation of |FRAME:CPD1F-132| is not only the first committed step towards the formation of the various steviol glucosides by a number of glucosyltransferases, but also an important divergence point away from gibberellin biosynthesis. There has been some controversy with regard to the characterization of |FRAME: EC-1.14.14.M48|. The enzyme was reported to have been purified to homogeneity from the stroma of |FRAME:TAX-55670| and shown to catalyze the formation of |FRAME: CPD-14501| from |FRAME: CPD1F-136| |CITS:[8806729]|. However, other authors, e.g. |CITS:[14561506]| |CITS:[17397883]| have argued that the nature of the reported enzyme is inconsistent with other oxidases in the pathway with regard to cell localization and that the reported N-terminal sequence was actually that of |FRAME: EC-4.1.2.13|. Various genes were later deposited in the Genbank database as encoding kaurenoic acid 13-hydroxylases, though none of them was characterized at the time. In 2016 the genes were tested for their activity by expression in an |FRAME: TAX-562| host, and only one of them was found to have activity, which was very low |CITS: [26358188]|. As of 2020, the highest activity for the conversion of |FRAME: CPD1F-136| to |FRAME: CPD-14501| is obtained with a recombinant |FRAME: TAX-3702| enzyme (CYP714A2) |CITS: [32013995]|. An enzyme similar to the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt; protein was identified in |FRAME: TAX-55670| and named KAHn2. However, a recombinant version of that enzyme still has a much lower activity than the Arabidposis enzyme |CITS: [26358188]|.</Description>
