    <DataNode elementId="CPD-14501" textLabel="steviol" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="QFVOYBUQQBFCRH-VQSWZGCSSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="600" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, O-28, hartmut">Stevia leaf extract is a non-nutritive sweetener derived from the plant |FRAME: TAX-55670|, which contains multiple sweet-tasting compounds called steviol glycosides. The Stevia leaf extract is increasingly used worldwide as natural, low-calorie sweetener that substitutes for sucrose to counteract growing incidence of obesity and diabetes. The two main steviol glycosides are |FRAME:CPD-14504| and |FRAME:CPD-14506|. The former is about 300 times sweeter than sucrose |CITS:[18556105][Lemus-Mondaca12]|. Unlike alternative sweeteners such as |FRAME: CPD-5581|, |FRAME:CPD-14504| and |FRAME:CPD-14506| are thermostable, making them suitable for use in cooked foods |CITS:[ Lemus-Mondaca12]|. Stevia leaves were used by indigenous people in South America as both a food sweetener and medicine. Stevia became more popular following its introduction in Japan in the 1970s. It was initially banned from use as a sweetener in many countries including the United States, Europe, Australia, Canada, and other countries, due to gaps in required safety information. In 1995 tt was permitted for use in the US only as a dietary supplement. Following the establishment of safety, metabolism, and clinical data |CITS:[18556105][19427890]|, purified steviol glycosides were approved for the use as sweetener by the FDA and many other national food safety authorities starting in 2008. Approval in the European Union and Canada followed in 2011 and 2012, respectively. Stevia leaf extract compounds are specifically approved for diabetes patients |CITS:[22492420][18555575][14561506]|. Steviol glucosides have also been reported to be effective in various &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; anticancer tests acting as chemopreventive agents for chemical carcinogenesis |CITS:[19131254]| and offer therapeutic benefits through anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory actions |CITS:[19000919]|. The steviol glucosides have been found to be efficient scavengers of reactive oxygen species (ROS) indicating their involvement in the antioxidant defense strategy of plants to thwart oxidative stress |CITS:[21043580]|. All of the sweet compounds in the Stevia leaf extract are derivatives of |FRAME: CPD-14501| and are degraded by gut bacteria back into steviol following ingestion. Steviol itself is completely resistant to bacterial degradation |CITS: [14558786]|.</Comment>
